  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 AM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nIt is an unseasonably mild early morning across the region, \nwith temperatures holding in the low to mid 60s (warmest in \nChicago) in most locations. However, a cold front is quickly \napproaching from eastern Iowa. Widely scattered showers and a \nfew embedded thunderstorms are ongoing ahead of the front within\na corridor of enhanced warm air advection. This activity will \npush eastward into northern Illinois through the predawn hours, \nthough coverage should diminish as it encounters some drier air \nover northeastern Illinois. Consequently, showers in the Chicago\nmetro area around daybreak are expected to remain hit or miss.\n\nAbnormally high dewpoints (mid to upper 50s) and modestly steep\nmid-level lapse rates ahead of the front may trigger renewed \nshower development by midday or early afternoon as the boundary \nshifts southward across the I-88 and I-80 corridors. Shower \ncoverage will likely remain low due to weak large-scale forcing \nand modest instability, but isolated to scattered activity is \nexpected to move east-southeast along the front this afternoon.\n\nWhile there is no strong push of cold air behind the front, \ntemperatures will turn cooler from north to south late this \nafternoon and evening, dropping into the 50s by sunset. A \nsurface high building into the western Great Lakes will bring a \nchilly, more seasonable night with efficient radiational \ncooling. Lows are forecast in the upper 30s to low 40s away from\nthe Chicago urban heat island.\n\nMore seasonable weather is expected into the weekend, \nparticularly Friday and Saturday, as onshore easterly flow, \nextensive cloud cover, and occasional rain persist. Highs will \ngenerally reach the 60s, though areas north of I-80 in northern \nIllinois may struggle to climb out of the 50s.\n\nAttention then turns to a conglomerated upper-level wave \nexpected to track from Mexico east-northeastward into the Lower\nGreat Lakes region during the weekend. There continues to be a \nstrong signal amongst model and ensemble guidance for a period \nof warm air advection (isentropic upglide) driven showers across\nour area, particularly Friday night through at least Saturday \nmorning. We also continue to see a signal for gradually waning \nshower coverage late in the afternoon hours (hopefully in time \nfor outdoor Halloween activities). Temperatures will be cooling \nback through the 50s after sunset.\n\nThe area looks to largely get a break in the coverage and \nchances of rain Saturday night and Sunday. However, there \ncontinues to be a strong ensemble signal for a large upper \ntrough to set up over the Plains into the Midwest next week. \nThis looks to support potentially several periods of wet \nweather across our area through much of the first work week of \nNovember.\n\nKJB",
